\subsection{Robustness Checks}
\label{sec:results-robustness}

See Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity-analyses} for the full output-length, compute-matched, single-context, and cross-family sensitivity analyses. Within the pre-specified output-length bands, \condAgenticCogWriter\ remains favored over \condSinglePass\ despite an overall tendency for the primary evaluator to prefer longer outputs. Under the tightest available compute match, the same favorable direction holds against \condStaged\ and \condTaskPlanning; no \condAgenticCogWriter--\condSinglePass\ pairs satisfy that band. Because output length and realized compute follow from execution, these checks are descriptive rather than causal.

Judge identity produces more variation than the resource-matched checks. Cross-family rescoring preserves the overall direction for the selected contrasts but yields many ties and reverses the WritingBench direction for \condAgenticCogWriter\ versus \condSinglePass. We therefore treat the cross-family result as partial robustness evidence, not proof that the exact win rates are evaluator-invariant.
